  "full_response": "TCP and UDP, standing for Transmission Control Protocol and User Datagram Protocol, are two distinct network protocols that are used for different kinds of communication on the internet. Here are the main differences:\n\n1. Connection Establishment: TCP is a connection-oriented protocol which means it establishes a connection between the data source and the destination before any data is sent. On the other hand, UDP is a connectionless protocol and does not establish a connection before sending data.\n\n2. Reliability: TCP is more reliable than UDP. It guarantees that no data is lost or corrupted during transmission. Each packet sent by TCP is independently numbered and is delivered in the same order it was sent. TCP also handles retransmission of lost packets and ensuring a correct sequence order. UDP does not provide these services, making it less reliable.\n\n3. Error Checking and Correction: TCP includes error checking and correction procedures, while UDP does not include error checking and correction. If TCP encounters an error, it will request the retransmission of the affected data. On the other hand, with UDP, error checking and must be manually handled by the sending and receiving programs.\n\n4. Speed and Efficiency: UDP is faster and more efficient than TCP since it does not have to establish connections, and does not perform any error checking or correction.\n\n5. Usage: TCP is typically used for streaming and data transfer, as it prioritizes reliability over speed. UDP, on the other hand, is used mostly for real-time applications like voice chat and video streaming where speed is more critical than reliability.\n\n6. Header Structure: TCP's header has many fields, including synchronization, acknowledgement, flags, partitioning information, windowing, and checksum. UDP's header, however, is simpler and only contains source and destination port numbers, sequence number (for TCP only), and checksum.\n\nIn summary, TCP is the protocol of choice for applications where reliable data transfer is more important than speed. UDP, on the other hand, is the protocol of choice for applications where speed is critical, and where occasional data loss or error is acceptable.",
